#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151938 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152150 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "e49f7799-2724-5fe0-999f-bfa9ab5ce610" :question/math-academy-id "e-6244" :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/4f/4faf932e63a57d2fe53fb3942cfe21c65d0d2a88692c665b2706bd68164855c9.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + M_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + M_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum,\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/26/26dc2a5af4c82657d9ec6fa331d4f5bc7575367bd4e3a00a5929b4ecaffe4477.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/6d/6d34737c12c35f6faec5caf37be500ba16c3f7cb18bf5ae660dc6d54182b66da.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,2)=5 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(2,2)=6 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(1,3)=7 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(2,3)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =5+6+7+8=26.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151939 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "1ac785c8-3d14-5968-94d3-6f4588f08d91" :question/math-academy-id "e-6245" :question/problem "Let $R = [1,5] \\times [2,6]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,3,5},\\,{P}_{2}={2,4,6},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/51/5133b73c3707b87725baeee9a34a64898d33056dab9d7d32ac8cc24dbe772321.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + M_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + M_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})=4$ for all $4$ regions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4\\cdot {M}_{11}+4\\cdot {M}_{21}+4\\cdot {M}_{12}+4\\cdot {M}_{22} \\\\  & =4({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/5b/5b711e2c33e8966dfc9f4274a8f556f712d51a450bbcd0cadb68771fd050d304.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/ad/ad83cf465252b1f880757405ca3ff9ab5e1b9b1e50d712bd3b81305b7973af27.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,2)=60 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(3,2)=52 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(1,4)=48 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(3,4)=40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4(60+52+48+40)=800.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151940 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152152 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/id #uuid "14e77998-b24b-56c1-8901-dc94d3ef98a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6246" :question/problem "![](images/33/33269c79413f4435d074d90cd727f2a53e7b9831300bfcaf0b5a7122df764080.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Find an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $2$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU = M_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = 8\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =8\\cdot {M}_{11}+4\\cdot {M}_{21} \\\\  & =8{M}_{11}+4{M}_{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152152 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73cfd878-a98b-5b6c-9768-415ec883dbbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{21}\\cdot {M}_{12}\\cdot {M}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254693} {:answer/id #uuid "b2789144-b138-5b29-8c59-3cd2f01c8608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}-{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}-{M}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255240} {:answer/id #uuid "b318e2a5-8a46-5471-a605-38962b9183ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255250} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1c3971-07ec-5035-aa02-5ecbed3c3494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255474} {:answer/id #uuid "f46a4e73-f0dd-51bd-ab04-3849b4463901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+2{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f46a4e73-f0dd-51bd-ab04-3849b4463901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+2{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255810} :answer-field/id #uuid "b112ebe7-07df-5f7c-8dd0-20f37949ebb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bab7c56-0d70-56a8-b7e8-20549b73d742" :question/math-academy-id "q-122847" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c28d3a6098da6c8be022974e96b5242e3bed58ef1347d92571b49c7b521a3b0d.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=\\text{Area}({R}_{22})=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =2\\cdot {M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+2\\cdot {M}_{12}+{M}_{22} \\\\  & =2{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+2{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152152 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d19970f-bec7-5c20-b651-c4d0064d68a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253782} {:answer/id #uuid "7f76ae9c-4d43-5dd2-b923-6b7da279bd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{M}_{11}+2{M}_{21}+4{M}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254797} {:answer/id #uuid "83204b2d-b8b3-5b6d-9928-ddea07e11ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+2{M}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254835} {:answer/id #uuid "832c78c0-5f67-50ca-94e6-c941a28b95ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+3{M}_{21}+2{M}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254837} {:answer/id #uuid "a125559f-9ad1-5393-b453-387d4afbbf6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48{M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{21}\\cdot {M}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f76ae9c-4d43-5dd2-b923-6b7da279bd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{M}_{11}+2{M}_{21}+4{M}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254797} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ae87587-d56d-5c95-b689-726e82283fb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df57e019-b35b-55c6-b523-1f5636c88e0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-123049" :question/problem "![](images/76/76654da8bebc4a156a55bcb6cc7411f4977dfa14634fbceb6e1a7f8e32b2d5b0.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=6, \\\\ \\text{Area}({R}_{21})=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{31})=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =6\\cdot {M}_{11}+2\\cdot {M}_{21}+4\\cdot {M}_{31} \\\\  & =6{M}_{11}+2{M}_{21}+4{M}_{31}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152150 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c582835-b1b3-5186-81fd-6f865e65bee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254488} {:answer/id #uuid "90d2434c-de15-5c77-97e7-de9a107f4cc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254941} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec8bffa-dd97-56c0-8381-ae4c75c4d634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255068} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5d3bc0-2146-53e7-bbc1-4880587e1249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255486} {:answer/id #uuid "f93feeae-79c1-5460-b446-177cf4275390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "450" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90d2434c-de15-5c77-97e7-de9a107f4cc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254941} :answer-field/id #uuid "deb902b7-3b48-5516-ad6d-401a1adec65e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e4225c9b-f9c3-509c-8faa-46e65d5c864c" :question/math-academy-id "q-123132" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,7]\\times [1,6]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=xy.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,3,5,7},\\,{P}_{2}={1,6},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/68/68270610704b1ef132469193e7948f54e548fa22223b6fc49b693753f998f874.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=10$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =10\\cdot {M}_{11}+10\\cdot {M}_{21}+10\\cdot {M}_{31} \\\\  & =10({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=xy$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=xy$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/76/7611c64cc460db725893156f6043644b34962a04f2aacef0b19cf99c5769cda3.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/75/75f378700ce6593a7ed9577b34e093cbbfe702e9512e9962e0d6f7f681694842.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(3,6)=18 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(5,6)=30 \\\\ {M}_{31} & =f(7,6)=42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =10(18+30+42) \\\\  & =900.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152150 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aae757b2-ad20-5f73-bc19-a111718bcefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148920} {:answer/id #uuid "b8976c57-12da-5ea3-be35-6374777492d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "448" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148921} {:answer/id #uuid "bf66b110-4f82-502b-9721-4f858b3f85b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148922} {:answer/id #uuid "e24febc7-0459-5c41-a48b-36604cec9050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148923} {:answer/id #uuid "fcab21c2-a85a-5473-a3de-68f4de5e1079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148924}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8be62ba8-d31a-5784-9045-50df8ca84da1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148919}] :question/id #uuid "c4585e46-b2c8-5baf-a8e2-9d0a8e6d3871" :question/math-academy-id "q-123133" :question/problem "Let $R = [2, 10] \\cdot [3, 7]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = x + y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {2, 6, 10}, P_{2} = {3, 5, 7}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152150 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b76a90e1-0093-5557-bfa6-336787d0228e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150780} {:answer/id #uuid "d60e774d-104e-5f8d-9cc4-b20acaef5d09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150781} {:answer/id #uuid "e53e2ada-6d52-5dda-b9e3-37cce39a3c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150782} {:answer/id #uuid "510f1120-7e20-58a5-be36-a89b41dbdb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150783} {:answer/id #uuid "72b0f521-a660-51fd-ae7d-ee19d27ee7e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150784}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8ba5c66a-45bc-5d4b-a390-8fdb670f2bcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150779}] :question/id #uuid "122fa252-520a-5995-91e9-6f1ba7a38a89" :question/math-academy-id "q-75047" :question/problem "Let $R = [2, 4] \\cdot [2, 4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = x^{2} + y^{2}$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {2, 3, 4}, P_{2} = {2, 3, 4}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d5155d4-5040-517e-8e8f-7971ed434882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343175} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe7d86e-6a6d-5c6e-bae8-62d02e24d8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343200} {:answer/id #uuid "76832c99-bebb-5fe0-9157-575668ce3e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343267} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5f54b4-2295-5778-842c-65970f6040aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343466} {:answer/id #uuid "c13e018e-5b18-5446-a8c8-958ec0a8570c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c5f54b4-2295-5778-842c-65970f6040aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343466} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f92efe2-b923-5ea7-87e1-4e0f30143a91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06416db6-a5ef-5504-be7c-78a29bd00e0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75048" :question/problem "Let $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=5-3x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={-1,0,1},\\,{P}_{2}={-1,0,1},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/85/859d9d6b15dbd08b4c015816e46986268f92a94770df866a562f3e379bae5365.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=5-3x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=5-3x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/4c/4c688c7cf529800b8f1ad075dc70784e40a59163330031b375eaad4b1cdd64c9.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/62/6269ba3c4fd853fff89327ab9e6968e2f52439f5c86c0ec5a21dce1620d9ba5d.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(-1,-1)=9 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(0,-1)=6 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(-1,0)=8 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(0,0)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =9+6+8+5 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152150 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8708a310-63f6-55bf-ba18-004dbc3b3fc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343404} {:answer/id #uuid "8b946381-7ae9-5ac1-954e-d5121e8be183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343453} {:answer/id #uuid "94ccb26e-518a-59f6-a3d4-664210b5c62a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343537} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0a6b5e-6522-5f68-9fb2-b6d5f06b09ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "152" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343882} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6ae120-ca4d-50ce-aa69-0b0b03035853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb0a6b5e-6522-5f68-9fb2-b6d5f06b09ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "152" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343882} :answer-field/id #uuid "64b9b0ff-fd08-5eb8-b6af-63a9135b0943" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac66a4bf-23ac-55ea-9e60-f731c8811568" :question/math-academy-id "q-75186" :question/problem "Let $R=[3,5]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={3,4,5},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/ed/ed8a998b543cb951a12599de55004c9220a53ac252a9eea66577475e2b8b6093.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-2$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-2$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/a0/a0033237f985416771125780a3d9b68bdfd8c6c33b9a4b0f2a32fa7af4c59bca.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/26/26cae8b6e2c69c2c0c66d78eb64c5d02fc500d10a970e54b09f6ee72bc145b74.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(4,2)=26 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(5,2)=35 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(4,3)=41 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(5,3)=50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =26+35+41+50 \\\\  & =152.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a00637db-de9b-56ed-a098-59411cde942a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150822} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a55e44-b375-54dd-a983-e6ed8acae1ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150823} {:answer/id #uuid "b92a08bf-50c4-51af-847d-a2e83b44b73f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150824} {:answer/id #uuid "6922a0cc-0640-5694-b856-0f882a575714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150825} {:answer/id #uuid "7eea493d-44d4-59ee-941b-3b758b85f0e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "104" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150826}] :answer-field/id #uuid "14edd6b1-b94a-57fc-9aaf-d788b0b6d33e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150821}] :question/id #uuid "27fbd536-fbac-5629-a345-3477109a83e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-75229" :question/problem "Let $R = [- 2, 2] \\cdot [- 2, 2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 6 - 2x - y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {-2, 0, 2}, P_{2} = {-2, 0, 2}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6166967f-21cd-5401-8c32-563682cb17fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343063} {:answer/id #uuid "71645f0c-15cc-51be-8849-56f4420f30a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343213} {:answer/id #uuid "d1dda424-2df2-5d85-a0b9-32f76281a943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344080} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a94b03-8710-5c33-af7f-efa5b8c6aa1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344086} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c86572-eaae-5431-8329-bed055c2ba6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6166967f-21cd-5401-8c32-563682cb17fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343063} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e38de4c-c69d-5f5a-8c75-137feef51656" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0513b4b6-45e5-5932-9fea-20a970dbf3e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-75232" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [2,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=30-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={2,3,4},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/b7/b7581f31a2031181aeb31bbdf8a3da6beff69b50ee41d5d73babbe198997fe15.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=30-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=30-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/df/dfed66710976869aa4507aea2e171c09ac248d4b8b4ca083972dbd10952af6b7.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/a6/a6366799e116eb35e8d3cb5401f0abc161ad183cd8f634bdaf2ce387f64d6c5c.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,2)=25 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(2,2)=22 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(1,3)=20 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(2,3)=17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =25+22+20+17 \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
